WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.867
Evidence is useful only when the organization can trust how it was collected and handled.

00:00:06.867 --> 00:00:13.317
In DFIR, evidence integrity means preserving information in a way that supports reliable analysis.

00:00:13.317 --> 00:00:26.842
That does not mean every case requires courtroom-grade procedures, but it does mean analysts should avoid unnecessary changes, document collection steps, and keep original sources separate from working copies whenever practical.

00:00:26.842 --> 00:00:34.541
Chain of custody is the documentation that shows how evidence moved from collection to analysis to storage.

00:00:34.541 --> 00:00:45.569
It should identify what was collected, when it was collected, where it came from, who collected it, who received it, and how it was protected.

00:00:45.569 --> 00:00:54.932
This documentation is especially important when incidents may involve regulatory reporting, employee investigations, insurance claims, litigation, law enforcement, or customer commitments.

00:00:54.932 --> 00:01:05.128
Common evidence sources include logs, disk images, memory captures, network data, cloud records, screenshots, notes, authentication events, email records, and administrative audit trails.

00:01:05.128 --> 00:01:07.833
Each source has different strengths.

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:13.451
Memory may reveal running processes or network connections that never touched disk.

00:01:13.451 --> 00:01:18.237
Disk images may preserve deleted files and operating system artifacts.

00:01:18.237 --> 00:01:24.271
Cloud audit logs may show identity activity that is invisible from the endpoint.

00:01:24.271 --> 00:01:30.305
A practical rule is to preserve before changing systems when the situation allows.

00:01:30.305 --> 00:01:39.669
Before wiping a host, rotating every credential, deleting a mailbox rule, or shutting down a workload, consider what evidence may disappear.

00:01:39.669 --> 00:01:44.871
During urgent containment, safety comes first, but even then notes matter.

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:49.657
Record what was changed, when it was changed, and why.

00:01:49.657 --> 00:01:56.939
Those details help later analysts understand the timeline instead of mistaking response activity for attacker activity.
